## [ISSUE] Per-client resource shadows the "all clients" permission

**Severity:** MEDIUM — admins holding an "all clients" permission silently lose it for any client that also has a per-client permission

### The Problem
```java
Resource resource =  resourceStore.findByName(server, client.getId(), server.getId());
if (resource == null) {
    resource = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);
    ...
}
Collection<Permission> permissions = root.evaluatePermission(new ResourcePermission(resource, resource.getScopes(), server), server);
```
The fallback to the `Clients` type resource happens only when no per-client resource exists. Once any permission targets client X, only X's own policies are evaluated for every admin.

### Theory / Proof
Per-client resources are created in `AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource` via `resourceStore.create(resourceServer, name, resourceServer.getClientId())` without `setType(...)`. In `DefaultPolicyEvaluator.evaluate` (server-spi-private/.../policy/evaluation/DefaultPolicyEvaluator.java:70-88) type-level policies are only pulled in when `resource.getType() != null`, and `findByScopes(resourceServer, null, scopes)` uses `findPolicyIdByNullResourceScope`, which requires `pe.resources is empty`. The all-clients permission is bound to the `Clients` resource (AuthorizationProvider.java:323-326), so it matches neither path when X's resource is evaluated.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Today the tests only grant either a per-client permission or an all-clients permission, never both, so it passes. Scenario: admin A gets `Clients / all / manage`; admin B gets `Clients / myclient / view`. After B's permission is created, `canManage(myclient)` for A evaluates only B's permission, which denies A, and A gets 403 on `myclient` while still able to manage every other client. The same pattern exists in `UserPermissionsV2.hasPermission`, so this PR copies a latent defect to a new resource type.

### Research
V1 `ClientPermissions` never had this problem because it had no all-clients concept. Keycloak's generic evaluator solves type-level + instance-level combination by giving instance resources a `type` (see `ClientPermissions.initialize`, which calls `resource.setType("Client")`).

### Recommendation
Evaluate both resources and OR the result, e.g.:
```java
Resource typeResource = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);
Resource resource = resourceStore.findByName(server, client.getId());
return (resource != null && granted(resource, scope)) || (typeResource != null && granted(typeResource, scope));
```
Alternatively set the resource type on per-object resources in `getOrCreateResource` so `DefaultPolicyEvaluator` pulls in type-level policies. Add a test that combines an all-clients and a single-client permission for different admins.

## [GAP ANALYSIS] New schema resource type and scopes are not provisioned for already-initialised realms

**Severity:** MEDIUM — realms that enabled admin permissions before this change can never create Clients permissions

### The Problem
```java
ClientModel client = realm.getAdminPermissionsClient();
if (client != null) {
    return;
}
```
Scopes and the per-type "all" resources are created only on first initialisation (lines 237-254). The PR adds a new resource type and three new scopes but no path that adds them to an existing admin-permissions resource server.

### Theory / Proof
`getScope` (line 205-217) throws `ModelValidationException("Scope [configure] does not exist.")` when the scope was never created. `getOrCreateResource` throws `ModelException("No scopes found.")` at line 108 for the same reason. For all-clients permissions, `getResourceTypeResource` returns null (AuthorizationProvider.java:323-327) so the permission is created with no resource. `init` is only invoked on realm creation (RealmManager.java:572) and when the `adminPermissionsEnabled` attribute is updated to true (JpaClientProviderFactory.java:69-75), both of which hit the early return.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Works today for freshly created realms, which is what the tests use. Any dev/test realm that had FGAP v2 enabled before this commit (the feature is experimental, so such realms exist on nightly builds) cannot create Client permissions without disabling admin permissions and deleting the `admin-permissions` client.

### Research
Keycloak normally handles schema evolution with migrations (`org.keycloak.migration.migrators`). Since the feature is experimental a migration may be overkill, but `init` could reconcile idempotently.

### Recommendation
Make `init` reconcile missing scopes and type resources instead of returning:
```java
if (client != null) {
    ResourceServer rs = authz.getStoreFactory().getResourceServerStore().findByClient(client);
    ensureScopesAndTypeResources(session, rs); // create any scope / type resource missing from SCHEMA
    return;
}
```
or document that realms must be re-initialised while the feature is experimental.

## [ISSUE] getClientsWithPermission can only return the "Clients" type resource name

**Severity:** LOW — latent: returns the type resource name instead of client IDs, which NPEs in the only caller if reached

### The Problem
```java
resourceStore.findByType(server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE, resource -> {
    if (hasGrantedPermission(resource, scope)) {
        granted.add(resource.getName());
    }
});
```

### Theory / Proof
Only the type resource created in `AdminPermissionsSchema.init` has `type = "Clients"` (lines 247-252). Per-client resources are created in `getOrCreateResource` via `resourceStore.create(...)` with no `setType`, so `findByType` never returns them. The result is `{"Clients"}` or empty. The caller `AvailableRoleMappingResource.getRoleIdsWithPermissions` does `realm.getClientById(cid).getRolesStream()`, which NPEs on `getClientById("Clients")`.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Today the caller is only reached when V1 `ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ` is enabled (AvailableRoleMappingResource.java:217), which is mutually exclusive with V2, so this is latent. It breaks as soon as that guard is updated to cover V2.

### Research
V1 `ClientPermissions.getClientsWithPermission` works because V1 resources are created with `setType("Client")` and a `client.resource.` name prefix it strips.

### Recommendation
Either throw `UnsupportedOperationException` like the other V2-unsupported methods until it is implemented, or enumerate per-client resources correctly and expand the all-clients grant:
```java
if (hasPermission(scope)) return realm.getClientsStream().map(ClientModel::getId).collect(toSet());
// otherwise iterate policies on the Clients type and collect their per-client resource names
```

## [GAP ANALYSIS] getResourceName does not handle the new Clients resource type

**Severity:** LOW — policy resource listing shows null names for Clients permissions

### The Problem
```java
if (USERS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
    ...
    return user.getUsername();
}
...
return resource.getDisplayName();
```

### Theory / Proof
`PolicyResourceService` (services/.../authorization/admin/PolicyResourceService.java:218) uses this for `displayName`. Client resources are created with name = client UUID and no display name, so `getDisplayName()` returns null. The `Clients` type resource created in `init` also has no display name.

### What happens today vs. what will break
The admin console shows a blank or UUID label for client permissions, while user permissions show usernames and "All users".

### Research
Mirror the Users branch.

### Recommendation
```java
} else if (CLIENTS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
    if (resource.getName().equals(CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE)) return "All clients";
    ClientModel client = session.clients().getClientById(session.getContext().getRealm(), resource.getName());
    if (client == null) throw new ModelIllegalStateException("Client not found for resource [" + resource.getId() + "]");
    return client.getClientId();
}
```
